P6 TTS — register `:feature:tts` into the Android host build
============================================================

Task:   t-muexn66v-0z6jje — "P6：TTS（Android TTS + MediaSession/前台服务）"
Module: android/feature/tts  (com.koodoreader.feature.tts, Android library)
Base:   commit 9abe7904 (branch task/p6-tts-t-muexn66)

WHY A PATCH INSTEAD OF THE EDITS
--------------------------------
The P6 execution constraints forbid this change from touching
android/settings.gradle, android/build.gradle, android/app/build.gradle (and
package.json / main.js). The module therefore ships unregistered, with its
gradle plugin ids resolved from the root `plugins { … apply false }` block that
hunk 1 adds. Apply the patch in the integration step:

    cd android && git apply -3 ../docs/patches/p6-tts.patch
    # or hand-apply the four hunks (they are small and self-explanatory)

Then verify:

    gradle :feature:tts:testDebugUnitTest        # needs the Android SDK (local.properties)
    gradle :app:assembleDebug -Ptarget=native

SCOPE OF THE PATCH
------------------
1. android/build.gradle                    — declare `com.android.library` 8.2.2 (apply false)
2. android/settings.gradle                 — `include ':feature:tts'`
3. android/app/build.gradle                — `implementation project(':feature:tts')`
4. android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml — foreground-service permission + `mediaPlayback`
                                             type, POST_NOTIFICATIONS, the TTS_SERVICE
                                             `<queries>` entry (Android 11+ package
                                             visibility) and the service/receiver declarations
5. src/assets/locales/en.json (optional)   — the four new i18n keys; run
                                             `node scripts/sync-locales-android.js` afterwards
                                             (ADR-004) so :core:common carries them on Android

NOT part of the patch, on purpose:
  * `:engine:toc` — `:feature:tts` links the reading position through its own
    `TtsReadingPosition`, which mirrors `ReadingPosition` field-for-field, so it stays
    buildable while the P2 TOC module is uncommitted. Wiring it later is a one-line
    adapter (docs/p6-tts-semantics-mapping.md §5).
  * `:engine:text` (P5) and `:engine:feature` (P6) are already included at the base
    commit, and are the module's only project dependencies.

The patch is hand-authored (no local Gradle/SDK run was possible in the worktree:
there is no gradlew script and the SDK is not usable from this session), so treat
the hunk line numbers as advisory and prefer `git apply -3` or manual application.

HOST INTEGRATION CHECKLIST (after the patch)
--------------------------------------------
1. `gradle :feature:tts:testDebugUnitTest` — 55 JVM unit tests (no Robolectric, no
   emulator; pure-logic files only, see docs/p6-tts-semantics-mapping.md §6).
2. Bind from the reader host:
   ```kotlin
   bindService(Intent(this, ForegroundTtsService::class.java), conn, BIND_AUTO_CREATE)
   // onServiceConnected:
   val tts = (binder as ForegroundTtsService.LocalBinder).service()
   tts.setTranslate { key -> localization.t(key) }          // :core:common Localization
   tts.setNotificationIcon(R.drawable.ic_stat_koodo)        // host icon (framework icon by default)
   tts.setReadingPositionProvider { myPositionAsTtsReadingPosition() }
   tts.setHostCommandListener { cmd -> if (cmd == TtsMediaCommand.NEXT) reader.nextChapter() }
   tts.loadChapterFromSource(bookKey, bookTitle, chapterTitle, chapterTextSource) { bytes ->
       TextDecoder.decode(bytes)                            // :engine:text
   }
   tts.play()
   ```
3. Render `TtsControlSheet(tts.controlState(), tts::command /* … */)` from the reader UI
   and pass `translate = localization::t` (i18n keys are the desktop ones).
4. Android 13+: request POST_NOTIFICATIONS before the first listening session, otherwise
   the foreground notification is present but the lock-screen card stays hidden.
5. Keep `:feature:tts` out of the WebView track if desired — the module has no side
   effects until the service is bound/started.

Everything below this line is the patch itself; nothing follows the last hunk.


--- a/android/build.gradle
+++ b/android/build.gradle
@@ -1,5 +1,9 @@
 // Root build file for the Android host of Koodo Reader.
 // Declares plugin versions only; the :app module applies them.
 plugins {
     id 'com.android.application' version '8.2.2' apply false
+    // Android library modules (e.g. :feature:tts — P6 TTS: TextToSpeech, MediaSession,
+    // foreground service, DataStore, Compose). Same AGP version as the application
+    // plugin above; without this line a subproject cannot apply 'com.android.library'.
+    id 'com.android.library' version '8.2.2' apply false
     id 'org.jetbrains.kotlin.android' version '1.9.24' apply false
--- a/android/settings.gradle
+++ b/android/settings.gradle
@@ -61,3 +61,12 @@
 // mapping / snapshot exporter are all algorithm-only, unit-tested without
 // the Android SDK. The Compose WebView host (engine/pdfhost) lives in :app.
 include ':engine:pdf'
+
+// TTS feature module (P6): Android TextToSpeech engine enumeration + voice labels,
+// DataStore-persisted rate/pitch/volume, MediaSession media-key transport,
+// foreground-service keep-alive for background listening, reading-position resume
+// and the lock-screen control UI skeleton.
+// Android library (it owns a Service / MediaSession / DataStore / Compose surfaces)
+// whose decision logic is pure JVM with JVM-only unit tests — see
